{"title":"2006 AIME II Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Burnside&#39;s_Lemma)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Burnside's Lemma)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(stars_and_bars)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (stars and bars)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Brute_Force_Casework)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Brute Force Casework)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Burnsides_for_Beginners)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Burnsides for Beginners)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There is an unlimited supply of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Congruent\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Congruent\">congruent</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Equilateral_triangle\" title=\"Equilateral triangle\">equilateral triangles</a> made of colored paper. Each <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a> is a solid color with the same color on both sides of the paper. A large equilateral triangle is constructed from four of these paper triangles. Two large triangles are considered distinguishable if it is not possible to place one on the other, using translations, rotations, and/or reflections, so that their corresponding small triangles are of the same color.\n</p><p>Given that there are six different colors of triangles from which to choose, how many distinguishable large equilateral triangles may be formed?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d14bcc3857fcbc46b474b626f03db304679edb5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A,B; A=(0,0); B=(2,0); pair C=rotate(60,A)*B; pair D, E, F; D = (1,0); E=rotate(60,A)*D; F=rotate(60,C)*E; draw(C--A--B--cycle); draw(D--E--F--cycle); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"218\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If two of our big equilateral triangles have the same color for their center triangle and the same <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multiset\" title=\"Multiset\">multiset</a> of colors for their outer three triangles, we can carry one onto the other by a combination of rotation and reflection.  Thus, to make two triangles distinct, they must differ either in their center triangle or in the collection of colors which make up their outer three triangles.\n</p><p>There are 6 possible colors for the center triangle.\n</p><p><br />\n<b>Case 1: All three outer triangles have different colors.</b>\n</p><p>We can choose the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> colors for the outer triangles out of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> total colors in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/b/62b47e04a4e8c7ad9f5064f255826fe7e0458f40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${6 \\choose 3} = 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"79\" height=\"53\" /> possible choices.\n</p><p><b>Case 2: Two outer triangles are one color and the third is a different color.</b>\n</p><p>We need to choose the two colors for the triangles, then consider a color can be put on two outer triangles or the one outer triangle for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/2/c424303bd198cc4f48b9c5d15aff752d3148feec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 {6 \\choose 2} = 6 \\cdot 5 = 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"146\" height=\"55\" /> ways.\n</p><p><b>Case 3: All three outer triangles are the same color.</b>\n</p><p>We choose one color for all three outer triangles in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/daf82293350752d15216995a13630370f8e9f960.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${6\\choose 1} = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"72\" height=\"55\" /> possible choices.\n</p><p><br />\nThus, in total we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/3/ec3caa8a353f8fa246bcdaafd01146454c7fbf22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot(20 + 30 + 6) = \\boxed{336}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"189\" height=\"23\" /> total possibilities.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Burnside.27s_Lemma.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Burnside's_Lemma)\">Solution 2 (Burnside's Lemma)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Burnside&#39;s Lemma)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We apply <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Burnside%27s_Lemma\" title=\"Burnside&#39;s Lemma\">Burnside's Lemma</a> and consider 3 rotations of 120 degrees, 240 degrees, and 0 degrees. We also consider three reflections from the three lines of symmetry in the triangle. Thus, we have to divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/f/6df768b2b37bfea85c62dfd671b8578ed1a76872.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+3=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> for our final count. \n</p><p>Case 1: 0 degree rotation. This is known as the identity rotation, and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/5/4957864295116ea677c600fdd2ea91cb9deaa637.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6^4=1296$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> choices because we don't have any restrictions.\n</p><p>Case 2: 120 degree rotation. Note that the three \"outer\" sides of the triangle have to be the same during this, and the middle one can be anything. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/2/ac257a3bf0b27302b484d59955d6b896b8d09192.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6*6=36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"80\" height=\"14\" /> choices from this. \n</p><p>Case 3: 240 degree rotation. Similar to the 120 degree rotation, each must be the same except for the middle. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/2/ac257a3bf0b27302b484d59955d6b896b8d09192.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6*6=36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"80\" height=\"14\" /> choices from this.\n</p><p>Case 4: symmetry about lines. We multiply by 3 for these because the amount of colorings fixed under symmetry are the exact same each time. Two triangles do not change under this, and they must be the same. The other two triangles (1 middle and 1 outer) can be anything because they stay the same during the reflection. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/0/7603d077d364b4bd78128e7328dfa98aa5396a09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6*6*6=216$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"112\" height=\"13\" /> ways for one symmetry. There are 3 symmetries, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/8/b98d9f4d17bb2b221d4d584a4a7989f869853c2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$216*3=648$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"104\" height=\"13\" /> combinations in all.\n</p><p><br />\nNow, we add our cases up: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/0/c3010d54bdf5be473775aa329fcb254a5c588b57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1296+36+36+648=2016$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"226\" height=\"14\" />. We have to divide by 6, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/7/ed7f053a8405bbb4a224daec30191e36065e8c0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2016/6=\\boxed{336}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"23\" /> distinguishable ways to color the triangle.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28stars_and_bars.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(stars_and_bars)\">Solution 3 (stars and bars)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (stars and bars)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> choices for the center triangle. Note that given any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> colors, there is a unique way to assign them to the corner triangles. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> different colors to choose from, so the number of ways to color the corner triangles is the same as the number of ways to arrange <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/d/74dbf5a4d426fb8bb63d88ab4f21b351d2ee670c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 - 1 = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"73\" height=\"13\" /> dividers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical items. Therefore, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/f/24f09a7dd6c04d85b85ee42bb0172d8950f282d9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[6 \\binom{5 + 3}{3} = 6\\binom{8}{3} = \\boxed{336}.\\]\" width=\"214\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>-MP8148\n</p><p>Explanation of the bijection by WIlliamgolly:\nLet 1,2,3,4,5,6 be the colors, and WLOG assume that the middle triangle has a color of 6.  Now, the color bijection can be formed as follows: Pick the colors to the immediate right of a divider.  If there is no color to the immediate right of a divider, then that color is 6.  \n</p><p>For example, ||12345| would represent the colors 1,1,6 as the colors chosen. Note for any three colors, there is only one way to fix it on the triangle, thus forming our stars and bars bijection.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Brute_Force_Casework.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Brute_Force_Casework)\">Solution 4 (Brute Force Casework)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Brute Force Casework)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If there is one color in total: there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways.\n</p><p><br />\nIf there are two colors in total:\n</p><p><br />\nSubcase 1: one color occupies 1 triangle and the other occupies 3 triangles:\n</p><p><br />\nSubsubcase 1: One color occupies the center triangle and the other occupies the three outer triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/6/1c689ec31c47ea2e3736b434f4bd27f88e5ad16d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot5=30$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" /> ways.\n</p><p>Subsubcase 2: One color occupies a corner triangle and the other occupies the three other triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/6/1c689ec31c47ea2e3736b434f4bd27f88e5ad16d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot5=30$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" /> ways.\n</p><p><br />\nSubcase 2: one color occupies 2 triangles and the other also occupies 2 triangles: this means one color must occupy the center and one outer triangle, the other color must occupy two outer triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/6/1c689ec31c47ea2e3736b434f4bd27f88e5ad16d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot5=30$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" /> ways.\n</p><p><br />\nIf there are three colors in total:\n</p><p>Subcase 1: one color occupies the center triangle, another occupies an outer triangle, the last color occupies the remaining two outer triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/e/82ed19bbface0670716c66c7fb9ed62f8680fbe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot5\\cdot4=120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"107\" height=\"14\" /> ways.\n</p><p>Subcase 2: one color occupies an outer triangle, another occupies an outer triangle, the last color occupies the center triangle and the remaining outer triangle: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/2/be2bdd6ef993a877a13af09aac1ad3d9a3af83ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\cdot10=60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" /> ways.\n</p><p><br />\nIf there are four colors in total:\n</p><p>Choose a color for the center triangle and choose three distinct colors from the five remaining colors to fill the three outer triangles: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/b/3eb5cec1cc78d8131878ed27836d1f6d5484ad18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\binom{5}{3}=60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"91\" height=\"55\" /> ways. \n</p><p><br />\nAdd the total number of ways from each case for the answer: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/12337d98462667c1064ffb48f11695bac8090e51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6+90+180+60=\\boxed{336}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"204\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>-unhappyfarmer\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Burnsides_for_Beginners.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Burnsides_for_Beginners)\">Solution 5 (Burnsides for Beginners)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Burnsides for Beginners)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider any coloring of a triangle. Inclusive rotations and reflections, we have 3 cases obtained per one case for rotations, and 3 cases obtained per one case for reflections. So, we divide all possible orderings by 6 possible rotations and reflections. These are called <b>orbits</b>, like the pack of gum.\n</p><p>Consider specific cases of partial (meaning not 360 degrees) rotations. We have a rotation by 0 degrees, called the identity case, which has 1296 possible colorings. We have a 120 degree rotation, called the cycle case (1). In this 120 degree rotation, we preserve the center of the grid, call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and swap the corners, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />. So, our cycle looks like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/3/3a3ab66e6423ec1839dc65e085e5640a154e8c02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)(D)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"88\" height=\"20\" />. Here, parentheses are nothing to be scared of. That just defines \"cyclic\". For each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, we have 6 cases, and for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, we have 6 cases. Therefore, we have 36 cases total. Now, considering 240 degrees, that again is the same as 120 degrees! So, another 36 cases arise.\n</p><p>Great! We have counted out our cyclic cases per ROTATION. Now, we must consider reflections. On the figure, we have three <b>lines of symmetry</b>, meaning we have 3 cases for reflection. When we reflect the figure, we swap two of the corner pieces while maintaining the third corner piece and the centre. That means, our cycle structure looks like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/b/2fbe8d41e3999940eebff92af60532ae46e5911e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(AB)(C)(D)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"102\" height=\"20\" />. There are 6 choices for each of the three, which is 216 choices total. (remember, because we account for BOTH rotations and reflections here, we can assume that this case will equal one of the previous rotating cases, as we divide by the orbits!) So, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d3ccda47c58681d964e178230ccb0c927fd576.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 216$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> total cases.\n</p><p>All in all, we have \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/f/d9fe4b59dac564a92b45de4b6219526e9a0d42b8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac1{6}\\Big(\\underbrace{1296}_{0^\\circ} +\\underbrace{36}_{120^\\circ} +\\underbrace{36}_{240^\\circ} +\\underbrace{216+216+216}_{\\text{3 reflections}}\\Big)\\]\" width=\"322\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>which we can quickly evaluate to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/8028e743d76b736d7c4bfc25ce542d5462ded067.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{336}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />. \n</p><p>For the case of this big triangle, the number of rotations and reflections upon this triangle is denoted as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/c/9dc1e74e6b3f0fac5b041f22732b163dbf57eaea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is called the <b>dihedral group</b>, and 3 represents the number of plausible rotations upon this group (of course, accounting reflections of course&#160;;) ).\n</p><p>The process of applying the above information to any given dihedral group <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2eacbcb991af87d495f8689ac0e952b51317dbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /> is called the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Burnside%27s_Lemma\" title=\"Burnside&#39;s Lemma\">Burnside's Lemma</a>.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfi23WXZIug&amp;list=PLa8j0YHOYQQIAMDRwkCILRZ5uHnIXcZiw\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfi23WXZIug&amp;list=PLa8j0YHOYQQIAMDRwkCILRZ5uHnIXcZiw</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II\" title=\"2006 AIME II\">2006 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2006 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2006 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2006\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td 